# Tutorial 10 - Choices and Built-in Menus
@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_10_menus
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Welcome to Tutorial 10 - Choices and Built-in Menus.
Lavender: This lesson is intentionally long-form, so your team can use it as a living reference.
Lavender: We'll cover guided sections, a dense reference sheet, and a mini practice scene.
Lavender: Focus area for this file: Choice design, menu flow, and consequence clarity.
Lavender: Primary command family: > Choice text -> label.
narrator: Keep this file editable. Teams usually annotate these lines with project-specific conventions.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: Choose a section. You can revisit any section in any order.
> Guided section index -> section_index
> Dense reference sheet -> reference_sheet
> Practice scene -> practice_scene
> Return to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

@label section_index
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: Guided sections:
> Section 01 -> section_01
> Section 02 -> section_02
> Section 03 -> section_03
> Section 04 -> section_04
> Section 05 -> section_05
> Section 06 -> section_06
> Section 07 -> section_07
> Section 08 -> section_08
> Section 09 -> section_09
> Section 10 -> section_10
> Back to lesson menu -> lesson_menu

@label section_01
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Section 01 for Choice design, menu flow, and consequence clarity.
Lavender: Intent: build a repeatable workflow, not one-off script tricks.
Lavender: Command anchor: > Choice text -> label.
narrator: Example line A01: [if var] gated choices
narrator: Example line B01: menu route returns and merges
Lavender: Read this as pattern language: setup, execute, validate, iterate.
Lavender: Tip 01: annotate why, not only what, in your own scripts.
Lavender: Team rule 01: keep naming and folder structures stable as assets grow.
Lavender: Review checkpoint 01: does this section still mirror runtime behavior?
